WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.134
Before buying hardware or installing tools, define what the lab is for.

00:00:04.134 --> 00:00:09.513
A lab for learning local model basics has different requirements than a lab for coding assistance,

00:00:09.513 --> 00:00:11.867
document analysis, image generation, vision testing, automation,

00:00:11.867 --> 00:00:13.547
retrieval-augmented generation, or application prototyping.

00:00:13.547 --> 00:00:17.582
Clear goals prevent expensive builds that look impressive but do not actually

00:00:17.582 --> 00:00:19.935
support the work you want to do.

00:00:19.935 --> 00:00:22.288
Requirements should include more than model speed.

00:00:22.288 --> 00:00:25.986
Think about data sensitivity, privacy expectations, budget, power use, cooling, noise,

00:00:25.986 --> 00:00:27.331
portability, uptime, and performance.

00:00:27.331 --> 00:00:31.365
A quiet mini PC may be better for everyday learning than a

00:00:31.365 --> 00:00:33.719
loud workstation that you avoid turning on.

00:00:33.719 --> 00:00:38.761
A laptop may be useful for travel even if it cannot run the largest models.

00:00:38.761 --> 00:00:43.804
A dedicated server may make sense only when the workload and operating expectations justify it.

00:00:43.804 --> 00:00:47.166
Scope also helps decide how formal the lab should be.

00:00:47.166 --> 00:00:51.537
If the lab handles only public test prompts, you may accept looser isolation.

00:00:51.537 --> 00:00:55.571
If it handles personal documents, client files, internal notes, or security-sensitive material,

00:00:55.571 --> 00:00:59.269
you need stronger data handling, backups, access control, and logging habits.

00:00:59.269 --> 00:01:01.622
Treat the lab as a data-handling environment,

00:01:01.622 --> 00:01:04.312
not just a toy machine with a GPU.

00:01:04.312 --> 00:01:06.665
A practical requirements statement can be simple.

00:01:06.665 --> 00:01:11.035
Name the primary use cases, the kind of data allowed in the lab,

00:01:11.035 --> 00:01:14.061
the expected users, the acceptable budget, the desired uptime,

00:01:14.061 --> 00:01:17.423
and the services that should be reachable from other devices.

00:01:17.423 --> 00:01:19.440
This gives you a decision filter.

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:22.130
When a new tool or hardware option appears,

00:01:22.130 --> 00:01:26.500
you can ask whether it serves the lab's purpose or simply adds complexity.
